"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,911\nThe reward system: learning from surprise\n\n3\n00:00:09,911 --> 00:00:16,575\nIn the 1990s, Wolfram Schultz and his colleagues recorded single dopamine neurons in monkeys.\n\n4\n00:00:16,934 --> 00:00:20,359\nAt first, a squirt of juice made the neurons fire.\n\n5\n00:00:20,806 --> 00:00:23,892\nThen the monkeys learned that a light came before the juice.\n\n6\n00:00:24,253 --> 00:00:26,321\nNow the neurons fired at the light,\n\n7\n00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:28,664\nand hardly changed when the juice arrived.\n\n8\n00:00:29,301 --> 00:00:31,024\nAnd when the juice failed to come,\n\n9\n00:00:31,256 --> 00:00:33,393\ntheir firing dipped below normal.\n\n10\n00:00:35,063 --> 00:00:37,945\nThese dopamine neurons sit in the midbrain,\n\n11\n00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:39,216\ndeep in the brain.\n\n12\n00:00:39,797 --> 00:00:43,556\nThey send signals forward to the striatum and the frontal lobes.\n\n13\n00:00:43,897 --> 00:00:47,642\nTogether, these areas are often called the reward system.\n\n14\n00:00:49,312 --> 00:00:51,004\nThe signal tracks surprise,\n\n15\n00:00:51,024 --> 00:00:52,325\nnot reward itself.\n\n16\n00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:54,684\nBetter than expected: a burst.\n\n17\n00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:57,626\nExactly as expected: no change.\n\n18\n00:00:57,896 --> 00:00:59,715\nWorse than expected: a dip.\n\n19\n00:01:00,189 --> 00:01:02,748\nThis is called a reward prediction error.\n\n20\n00:01:02,959 --> 00:01:04,758\nIt works like a teaching signal,\n\n21\n00:01:04,778 --> 00:01:07,397\ntelling the brain to update its predictions.\n\n22\n00:01:09,067 --> 00:01:11,819\nDopamine is often called the pleasure chemical,\n\n23\n00:01:12,038 --> 00:01:13,478\nbut that is misleading.\n\n24\n00:01:13,935 --> 00:01:20,000\nRats with almost all their dopamine removed still showed normal signs of enjoying sugar.\n\n25\n00:01:20,448 --> 00:01:23,169\nWhat they lost was the drive to go and get it.\n\n26\n00:01:23,679 --> 00:01:26,624\nDopamine is more about wanting than liking.\n\n27\n00:01:28,294 --> 00:01:30,674\nCuriosity uses the same system.\n\n28\n00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,955\nIn one study, people read trivia questions,\n\n29\n00:01:34,156 --> 00:01:35,918\nthen waited for the answers.\n\n30\n00:01:36,315 --> 00:01:37,903\nThe more curious they were,\n\n31\n00:01:38,096 --> 00:01:41,431\nthe more the midbrain and nucleus accumbens activated,\n\n32\n00:01:41,681 --> 00:01:43,870\nand the better they remembered the answers.\n\n33\n00:01:44,307 --> 00:01:48,046\nThey even remembered unrelated faces shown while they waited.\n\n34\n00:01:49,716 --> 00:01:51,738\nRewards can also backfire.\n\n35\n00:01:52,206 --> 00:01:58,282\nIn 1973, preschoolers who already liked drawing were promised a certificate for drawing.\n\n36\n00:01:58,821 --> 00:02:00,421\nLater, in free play,\n\n37\n00:02:00,661 --> 00:02:03,774\nthey drew less than children who got no award,\n\n38\n00:02:03,909 --> 00:02:05,059\nor a surprise one.\n\n39\n00:02:06,729 --> 00:02:08,425\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n40\n00:02:08,762 --> 00:02:11,160\nLearning happens when predictions are wrong.\n\n41\n00:02:11,634 --> 00:02:17,135\nAsk learners to predict before they find out,\n\n42\n00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:19,264\nand spark curiosity before giving answers.\n\n43\n00:02:19,615 --> 00:02:22,894\nAnd use rewards with care: a promised prize can crowd out real interest.\n\n44\n00:02:25,564 --> 00:02:28,171\nYour reward system learns from surprise.\n\n45\n00:02:28,493 --> 00:02:31,352\nWhen the world is better or worse than you predicted,\n\n46\n00:02:31,516 --> 00:02:32,492\nit updates.\n\n"
